      "textEn": "256\n\nGreetings. Meaning: The Vedic evidence refers to the word-Brahman. There are many things that are beyond the observation of our incomplete senses,\nbut the authentic evidence of the word is complete. The Vedas are called Shabda-Brahma, because\nthe proof of the Vedas is the ultimate knowledge. The reason for this is that the word-Brahma or Vedas represent God. But the real essence of the word-Brahman is the chanting of the Hare Krishna mantra. By the utterance of this divine\nsound, the meaning of all material and spiritual things is revealed. This Hare Krishna\nMahamantra is inseparable from God. The meaning of each object is revealed through the sound of words received through the air. This sound, whether physical or spiritual, but without sound, no meaning of anything can be understood. It is said in the Vedas—Antarbahishcha tatsarvam vyay narayanah sthitah—\n“Narayan is omnipresent and he is situated inside and outside.\n” This is confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita (13.34)—\n\nAs the light illuminates the entire world, so does the sun. The field, the knower of the field, thus illuminates the field, O Bharata. “Hey Bharatwanshi! Just as the sun alone illuminates this universe, similarly, the individual soul\nand the supreme soul illuminate the entire body through consciousness.\"\n\nIn other words, the consciousness of the soul and the Supreme Soul is omnipresent, the soul, from its limited consciousness,\nremains pervaded in the entire physical body, and the supreme consciousness of God remains pervaded in the entire universe. Since the soul is situated within the body, consciousness pervades the whole body. Similarly, the Supreme Being,\nor Shri Krishna, is pervading this universe, therefore every object functions in an organized manner. By the presiding deity,\nThe nature is manifested, the animate and inanimate—\"O son of Kunti! This nature is working under my direction and creates all the moving and non-moving beings.” (Bhagavad Gita 9.10).\nTherefore, Shivji prays to God that He may be merciful, so that only by chanting the Hare Krishna mantra,\nevery object in the physical and spiritual worlds can be understood. In this context, the word 'Amushmai'\nis important because it indicates the supreme goal after attaining the higher realm. The employees who are"
